The performance gap between reflection approaches is significant enough to matter in hot loops processing thousands of objects per second. Caching the `Method` object is the baseline optimization — repeated calls to `getDeclaredMethod()` walk class metadata tables and allocate new Method objects each time.

```java
// BAD: looks up Method on every call
for (Object obj : objects) {
    Method m = obj.getClass().getDeclaredMethod("getName");
    m.setAccessible(true);
    String name = (String) m.invoke(obj);
}

// GOOD: cache Method object, set accessible once
Method m = MyClass.class.getDeclaredMethod("getName");
m.setAccessible(true); // skip access checks on subsequent calls
for (Object obj : objects) {
    String name = (String) m.invoke(obj); // still boxes args
}
```

Caching `Method` eliminates lookup cost but `invoke()` still boxes primitives and prevents inlining. **MethodHandle** solves the inlining problem by integrating with the JVM's `invokedynamic` infrastructure, allowing the JIT compiler to treat the call as a virtual dispatch once the handle is bound.

```java
// MethodHandle: JIT-friendly, inlineable after warmup
MethodHandles.Lookup lookup = MethodHandles.lookup();
MethodHandle mh = lookup.findVirtual(MyClass.class, "getName",
        MethodType.methodType(String.class));
for (Object obj : objects) {
    String name = (String) mh.invoke(obj); // JIT can inline this
}
```

For the highest throughput, **LambdaMetafactory** generates a real functional interface at runtime. The one-time setup cost is higher, but every subsequent call executes at native speed because the JVM compiles the generated class like any other concrete implementation. Jackson 3.x and modern Spring use this technique internally for bean property access, which is why their reflection-heavy operations are faster than naive `Method.invoke` loops.

Compile-time annotation processing follows a round-based model. In each round, `javac` presents newly discovered annotated elements to processors. If a processor generates new source files containing annotations, those files trigger another round. Processing continues until no new files are generated — a fixed-point iteration.

```java
@SupportedAnnotationTypes("com.example.Validate")
@SupportedSourceVersion(SourceVersion.RELEASE_17)
public class ValidateProcessor extends AbstractProcessor {
    @Override
    public boolean process(Set<? extends TypeElement> annotations,
                           RoundEnvironment roundEnv) {
        for (Element el : roundEnv.getElementsAnnotatedWith(
                Validate.class)) {
            if (el.getKind() != ElementKind.FIELD) {
                processingEnv.getMessager().printMessage(
                    Diagnostic.Kind.ERROR,
                    "@Validate is only valid on fields", el
                );
            }
        }
        return true; // claim the annotation
    }
}
```

The processor receives each annotated element through the `RoundEnvironment` and can emit errors that fail the build, generate new source files via `processingEnv.getFiler()`, or produce resource files. Returning `true` from `process()` claims the annotation — no other processor will see it.

Runtime annotation reading follows a different path entirely. When Spring starts, it scans packages for classes annotated with `@Component` and its stereotypes. Each reflective call to `Class.getAnnotations()` returns proxy objects implementing the annotation interface. The overhead is measurable at startup — a large Spring application with 2,000 beans might spend 500ms just on annotation scanning. Spring caches everything into `BeanDefinition` objects so the running application never re-reads annotations.

Modern Spring versions also support **ahead-of-time (AOT) processing** that moves annotation scanning to build time, generating the equivalent metadata as source code — effectively converting runtime processing into compile-time processing for faster startup.
